<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ruleset name="parallel-image-processor"
    xmlns="http://pmd.sourceforge.net/ruleset/2.0.0"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://pmd.sourceforge.net/ruleset/2.0.0
        https://pmd.sourceforge.io/ruleset_2_0_0.xsd">

  <description>
    PMD rules for Parallel Image Processor (PMD 7, via maven-pmd-plugin 3.26.0).

    PMD overlaps Checkstyle in about a third of its rules and disagrees with it in a few.
    Where they overlap, the rule lives in exactly one of the two files, and the one that
    owns it is the one that reports it better: Checkstyle owns everything about how the
    source *looks* (naming, imports, braces, javadoc structure, line length), and this
    file owns everything about what the code *does*. That is why the codestyle category
    is almost absent below and why the multithreading, errorprone and design categories
    carry the weight.

    Like config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml, every rule here was switched on, run against
    src/main/java, and kept only if the code already passed it or the code was wrong and
    got fixed. Rules that fired on deliberate design are excluded *with the reason*, not
    silenced with a priority bump — a rule at a priority the build ignores is a comment
    with XML syntax.
  </description>

  <!-- Tests are excluded here as well as in the pom, for the same reason: a test that
       spins up a pool, asserts on a race, and names its methods in prose is not code
       PMD's design rules can usefully judge. -->
  <exclude-pattern>.*[/\\]src[/\\]test[/\\].*</exclude-pattern>
  <exclude-pattern>.*[/\\]target[/\\].*</exclude-pattern>

  <!-- ===================================================================
       Concurrency

       The reason this project exists. This is the one category taken almost whole,
       because a false positive here costs a comment and a missed true positive costs
       a heisenbug that reproduces once a fortnight on someone else's machine.
       =================================================================== -->
  <rule ref="category/java/multithreading.xml">
    <exclude name="DoNotUseThreads"/>
    <exclude name="AvoidSynchronizedStatement"/>
    <exclude name="AvoidSynchronizedAtMethodLevel"/>
    <exclude name="UseConcurrentHashMap"/>
    <exclude name="AvoidUsingVolatile"/>
  </rule>

  <!-- DoNotUseThreads is a Jakarta EE rule: in a managed container the application
       server owns thread creation, so `new Thread(...)` is a violation of the platform
       contract. This is a local desktop and CLI application that owns its own process
       and whose entire subject is a ForkJoinPool it constructs itself. The rule would
       fire on ImagePoolFactory, on the JavaFX shutdown hook, and on the daemon thread
       that drains progress events — three places where creating a thread is the design.

       AvoidSynchronizedStatement / AvoidSynchronizedAtMethodLevel argue for a
       java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock over intrinsic locking. That is good advice where
       a lock needs a timeout, a fairness policy, or tryLock, and it is exactly what
       ImageMetadataCache does — it holds a StampedLock precisely because optimistic
       reads matter there. Everywhere else the locking is a two-line critical section
       guarding one field, where `synchronized` is shorter, cannot leak a held lock past
       an early return, and needs no finally block to be correct. Swapping those for
       ReentrantLock would add the failure mode the rules exist to prevent.

       UseConcurrentHashMap fires on every HashMap regardless of whether it is shared,
       including the local maps built inside a single method and the immutable ones
       returned by Map.copyOf. What matters is publication, not the implementation
       class, and publication is what the multithreading rules kept below actually check.

       AvoidUsingVolatile is the odd one out: it is not merely noisy here, it is advice
       this project cannot take. It fired on ImageProcessingEngine.closed, a flag written
       by whichever thread calls close() and read by every submit() that must refuse work
       afterwards. The rule's suggested alternative — a synchronized block — would put a
       lock acquisition on the hot path to protect a single boolean, and volatile is
       precisely the primitive the memory model provides for that case. Cancellation and
       shutdown flags are the textbook correct use of volatile, and this engine has both. -->

  <!-- ===================================================================
       Error-prone constructs
       =================================================================== -->
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/AvoidBranchingStatementAsLastInLoop"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/AvoidDecimalLiteralsInBigDecimalConstructor"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/AvoidMultipleUnaryOperators"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/AvoidUsingOctalValues"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/BrokenNullCheck"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/CheckSkipResult"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/ClassCastExceptionWithToArray"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/CloseResource">
    <properties>
      <!-- The JDBC types are the point: pip-persistence hands out Connections from a
           Database facade, and a leaked Connection under SQLite is not a slow leak but
           an immediate "database is locked" for the next writer. -->
      <property name="types"
          value="java.sql.Connection,java.sql.Statement,java.sql.PreparedStatement,java.sql.ResultSet,java.io.InputStream,java.io.OutputStream"/>
      <property name="closeAsDefaultTarget" value="true"/>
    </properties>
  </rule>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/CompareObjectsWithEquals"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/ConstructorCallsOverridableMethod"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/DontUseFloatTypeForLoopIndices"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/EmptyCatchBlock">
    <properties>
      <!-- Same contract as Checkstyle's EmptyCatchBlock: a comment or a variable named
           `ignored` is what turns a swallowed failure into a stated decision. -->
      <property name="allowCommentedBlocks" value="true"/>
      <property name="allowExceptionNameRegex" value="^(ignored|expected)$"/>
    </properties>
  </rule>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/EqualsNull"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/IdempotentOperations"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/InstantiationToGetClass"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/JumbledIncrementer"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/MisplacedNullCheck"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/OverrideBothEqualsAndHashcode"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/ReturnEmptyCollectionRatherThanNull"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/SuspiciousOctalEscape"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/UnconditionalIfStatement"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/UnnecessaryConversionTemporary"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/UnusedNullCheckInEquals"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/UselessOperationOnImmutable"/>

  <!-- AvoidCatchingThrowable is deliberately absent, for the reason IllegalCatch is
       absent from checkstyle.xml: BatchProcessingTask catches Throwable at the task
       boundary so that one unreadable image cannot end a 4000-image batch, and the
       `catch (Error e) { throw e; }` clause immediately above it is what keeps an
       OutOfMemoryError from being swallowed. That ladder is the fix for the problem the
       rule looks for, and the rule cannot tell the difference. ARCHITECTURE §2.6 and the
       tests that assert a failing image yields a Failure outcome are what guard it.

       AvoidLiteralsInIfCondition is absent because it flags `if (radius == 0)` and
       `if (exitCode != 0)`, where naming the literal would obscure it.

       MissingSerialVersionUID is absent because nothing here is Serializable except
       RecursiveTask/RecursiveAction subclasses, which inherit serialisability from
       ForkJoinTask and are never actually serialised; a UID on them would be cargo. -->

  <!-- ===================================================================
       Best practices
       =================================================================== -->
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/AvoidReassigningCatchVariables"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/AvoidReassigningLoopVariables">
    <properties>
      <!-- `skip` allows the argv-parser idiom in CliOptions.parse, where the loop index
           is the parse cursor and a flag that takes a value must advance past it. The
           same decision as the ModifiedControlVariable entry in the Checkstyle
           suppressions file, expressed here as configuration because PMD offers it. -->
      <property name="foreachReassign" value="deny"/>
      <property name="forReassign" value="skip"/>
    </properties>
  </rule>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/AvoidUsingHardCodedIP"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/CheckResultSet">
    <properties>
      <!-- The rule looks for rs.next() in the condition of an `if` or a `while` and reports it
           otherwise. Database.applyPragmas checks it in a ternary instead —

               String mode = rs.next() ? rs.getString(1) : "unknown";

           — which checks the result just as thoroughly and keeps `mode` final-by-shape rather
           than a mutable variable pre-seeded with a default. Being the condition of a ternary
           is what the XPath asserts, so this cannot silently excuse an unchecked next(). -->
      <property name="violationSuppressXPath" value="./ancestor::ConditionalExpression"/>
    </properties>
  </rule>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/DoubleBraceInitialization"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/ForLoopVariableCount"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/MissingOverride"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/PreserveStackTrace"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/PrimitiveWrapperInstantiation"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/SimplifiableTestAssertion"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UnusedAssignment">
    <properties>
      <!-- Suppressed inside a record's compact constructor, where PMD 7.7.0 reports a
           false positive that is dangerous to act on. In

               public ImageMetadata {
                   exif = exif == null ? Map.of() : Map.copyOf(exif);
               }

           the assignment looks dead — nothing reads `exif` afterwards — but the compiler
           emits `this.exif = exif` for every component at the end of the block, so that
           line *is* the defensive copy. "Fixing" the warning by deleting it would publish
           a caller's mutable map into a value type shared across a batch. Two records are
           affected today (ImageMetadata, ProgressEvent) and every future one that
           normalises a component would be too, so the exception is expressed by AST
           location rather than by naming files. The rule keeps working everywhere else,
           which is where it earns its place: a value computed and then overwritten in a
           long method is exactly the bug it finds. -->
      <property name="violationSuppressXPath"
          value="./ancestor::CompactConstructorDeclaration"/>
    </properties>
  </rule>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UnusedFormalParameter">
    <properties>
      <!-- BatchProcessingTask and TileProcessingAction each add a writeObject(ObjectOutputStream)/
           readObject(ObjectInputStream) pair that unconditionally throws NotSerializableException,
           refusing serialisation outright rather than silently handing back a broken task (see each
           class's own javadoc). Java's serialization mechanism finds these methods by reflection on
           an exact private method signature — the parameter has to be there and has to keep that
           type for the JVM to recognise the hook at all, so "unused" here is not a sign the parameter
           can be dropped. -->
      <property name="violationSuppressXPath"
          value="./ancestor::MethodDeclaration[@Name='writeObject' or @Name='readObject']"/>
    </properties>
  </rule>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UnusedLocalVariable"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UnusedPrivateField"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UnusedPrivateMethod"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UseCollectionIsEmpty"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UseStandardCharsets"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UseTryWithResources"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/WhileLoopWithLiteralBoolean"/>

  <!-- LiteralsFirstInComparisons is absent, and it has to be, because it is PMD's name for the rule
       checkstyle.xml turns off as EqualsAvoidNull. Enabling one and disabling the other would mean the
       build's opinion on `method.equals("POST")` depended on which of the two tools happened to reach
       the file first — and this rule is what proved that had already happened: pip-app was invisible to
       PMD until pmd:check was run inside a lifecycle build that could resolve its sibling modules, so
       ten sites in it went unreported while the Checkstyle twin had been switched off for the same ten.

       The reasoning is the one written out in checkstyle.xml and it holds here without change. Every
       receiver PMD flagged is provably non-null: HttpExchange.getRequestMethod(), whose contract says
       so; the result of raw.trim(); a key drawn from ENV_ALIASES.keySet(); and a format string a
       validator has already matched against a fixed list. Neither rule has a "nullable receivers only"
       mode, so the yield of keeping it is ten inverted comparisons that read backwards and no null
       safety that validating at the edges does not already provide. StringLiteralEquality in
       checkstyle.xml stays on, and it is the half of this territory that finds real bugs: `==` between
       strings is never right. -->

  <!-- GuardLogStatement is absent, and this is a case where the codebase already does
       better than the rule asks. The rule wants `if (LOG.isLoggable(DEBUG))` around any
       log call that builds a string. Every log call here passes a Supplier<String> to
       System.Logger instead, so the string is never built unless the level is enabled —
       the same saving without the guard. PMD does not model that, and reports the
       lambda form as unguarded. -->

  <!-- ===================================================================
       Design

       Thresholds, like the Checkstyle size rules, are set above the largest thing in
       the codebase so they fire on a change of kind rather than a change of degree.
       =================================================================== -->
  <rule ref="category/java/design.xml/CollapsibleIfStatements"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/design.xml/SimplifiedTernary"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/design.xml/SimplifyBooleanReturns"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/design.xml/SwitchDensity"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/design.xml/UselessOverridingMethod"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/design.xml/CyclomaticComplexity">
    <properties>
      <property name="classReportLevel" value="120"/>
      <property name="methodReportLevel" value="20"/>
    </properties>
  </rule>
  <rule ref="category/java/design.xml/NPathComplexity">
    <properties>
      <property name="reportLevel" value="400"/>
    </properties>
  </rule>
  <rule ref="category/java/design.xml/ExcessiveParameterList">
    <properties>
      <property name="minimum" value="10"/>
    </properties>
  </rule>

  <!-- LawOfDemeter is absent. It reports a violation for nearly every fluent call in
       existence, including the builder chains this codebase uses on purpose.

       ExcessiveImports, TooManyMethods, TooManyFields and ExcessiveClassLength are
       absent because they measure size without regard to cohesion, and the two largest
       classes here — AppConfig and BatchJobController — are large because they are
       exhaustive about their input, which is the property that makes them worth having.
       CyclomaticComplexity above catches the thing those rules are proxies for. -->

  <!-- ===================================================================
       Performance

       Only the rules that describe a real cost in a per-pixel loop. Most of this
       category is folklore from JDK 1.4 and the JIT has since made it wrong.
       =================================================================== -->
  <rule ref="category/java/performance.xml/AddEmptyString"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/performance.xml/AppendCharacterWithChar"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/performance.xml/BigIntegerInstantiation"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/performance.xml/ConsecutiveAppendsShouldReuse"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/performance.xml/ConsecutiveLiteralAppends"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/performance.xml/InefficientEmptyStringCheck"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/performance.xml/InefficientStringBuffering"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/performance.xml/InsufficientStringBufferDeclaration"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/performance.xml/StringInstantiation"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/performance.xml/StringToString"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/performance.xml/UseArraysAsList"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/performance.xml/UseIndexOfChar"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/performance.xml/UselessStringValueOf"/>

  <!-- AvoidInstantiatingObjectsInLoops is absent, which for an image processor may look
       backwards. It is not: the rule cannot distinguish an allocation in a hot per-pixel
       loop from one in a loop over a list of jobs, and the allocations it would find here
       are all of the second kind — a Path per file, a JobOutcome per image. The per-pixel
       loops allocate nothing at all, and what keeps them that way is the JMH benchmarks
       in benchmarks/, not a lint rule that would be firing on the other loops so often
       that nobody reads it. -->

  <!-- ===================================================================
       Security
       =================================================================== -->
  <rule ref="category/java/security.xml"/>

  <!-- ===================================================================
       Code style — one rule only

       Checkstyle owns this territory. UnnecessaryImport is here rather than there
       only because PMD's version also understands static imports of nested types,
       which is where the Checkstyle equivalent has a blind spot.
       =================================================================== -->
  <rule ref="category/java/codestyle.xml/UnnecessaryImport"/>

</ruleset>
